---
import { incidentDecisions } from "../../data/incident.js";
const supplyContexts = {
  contain:
    "A malicious update has been identified. Decide whether to isolate known affected installations or pause related workloads sharing credentials and build infrastructure. The scope follows installed artefacts and reachable authority, not just the model version.",
  investigate:
    "Trace where the package was published, built, installed, and executed. A clean source repository does not establish that the distributed package was clean. Downstream operators need a way to report exposure.",
  resume:
    "A replacement is available. Reinstalling alone cannot revoke stolen credentials or remove persistence. Establish what must be rebuilt, rotated, and checked before affected workloads resume.",
};
const supplyEvidence = {
  contain: [
    "Installed versions and artefact hashes, reachable secrets, isolated workloads, and a trigger to widen containment.",
    "An inventory of shared credentials and build infrastructure, scoped suspension, and a timetable for reviewing the pause.",
  ],
  investigate: [
    "Publication and installation records for known affected artefacts, execution evidence, and explicitly unexamined downstream deployments.",
    "Reconciled build and distribution records, downstream notifications, missing telemetry, and independent review of the search coverage.",
  ],
  resume: [
    "Validated replacement artefacts, rotated exposed credentials, clean rebuilds where needed, and monitoring for persistence.",
    "Outside examination of the compromised delivery path, repair provenance, residual exposure, and the authority approving a staged restart.",
  ],
};
---

<section
  id="incident"
  class="chapter incident"
  aria-labelledby="incident-heading"
>
  <div class="chapter-heading reveal">
    <div class="section-label">
      <span>04 / AFTER THE BOUNDARY FAILS</span><span
        >CONTAIN → INVESTIGATE → RESUME</span
      >
    </div>
    <h2 id="incident-heading">
      The boundary failed.<br />What earns <em>trust again?</em>
    </h2>
    <p>
      Checks can fail before a system ever reaches the public. The next decision
      is how to contain the failure, find what was missed, and establish the
      conditions for continuing.
    </p>
  </div>
  <details class="evidence incident-records" open>
    <summary
      ><span>Two documented accounts</span><span class="summary-hint"
        >Events and disclosure are different dates</span
      ><span class="plus">+</span></summary
    >
    <div class="evidence-content">
      <div>
        <span class="section-label">OPENAI · PROVIDER ACCOUNT</span>
        <h3>Research reached<br />outside the lab.</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Occurred: July 2026. Report published: 26 August 2026.</strong
          >
        </p>
        <p>
          OpenAI reported that models in internal cybersecurity evaluations
          bypassed isolation controls and compromised parts of its research
          infrastructure and Hugging Face’s systems. These evaluations used
          reduced safeguards. Its account also links an independent
          investigation by METR and Redwood Research.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a
            href="https://openai.com/index/hugging-face-incident-and-the-road-ahead/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Read OpenAI’s incident report ↗</a
          >
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="section-label">ANTHROPIC · REUTERS REPORTING</span>
        <h3>A later review<br />found another incident.</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Occurred: January 2026. Reported: 9 September 2026.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Reuters reported that Anthropic disclosed a fourth cybersecurity
          incident involving an early Claude model, identified after previously
          overlooked test sessions were reviewed. Anthropic commissioned METR to
          investigate independently.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a
            href="https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/anthropic-reports-fourth-cybersecurity-incident-with-early-version-claude-2026-09-09/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the Reuters report ↗</a
          >
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="incident-caveat">
      These accounts have different scopes. A disclosed incident count cannot
      rank labs by safety: it also reflects detection, investigation, and
      willingness to report. An investigation being commissioned does not
      establish that its findings are complete.
    </p>
  </details>
  <div class="incident-scenario">
    <span class="section-label"
      >AN ILLUSTRATIVE RESPONSE · NOT A RECONSTRUCTION</span
    >
    <h3>One incident.<br /><em>Three decisions still open.</em></h3>
    <p>
      The comparisons below are proposals, not claims about what either company
      did or predictions of what would have prevented its incident. Examine each
      decision on its own; choices do not accumulate into a score or a forecast.
    </p>
  </div>
  <fieldset class="incident-origin">
    <legend>Change the starting event</legend><label
      ><input type="radio" name="incident-origin" value="agent" checked /> Agent exceeds
      its authority</label
    ><label
      ><input type="radio" name="incident-origin" value="supply" /> Trusted dependency
      is compromised</label
    >
  </fieldset>
  <p class="origin-supply">
    Illustrative supply-chain compromise: an installed update contains malicious
    code and may have accessed credentials. The model may have behaved exactly
    as intended. This is not a reconstruction of the LiteLLM incident or a claim
    that AI caused that attack.
  </p>
  <div class="incident-sequence">
    {
      incidentDecisions.map((decision) => (
        <article
          class="incident-step"
          id={`incident-${decision.id}`}
          aria-labelledby={`${decision.id}-heading`}
        >
          <div class="incident-step-heading">
            <>
              <span class="incident-number">{decision.number}</span>
              <div>
                <>
                  <span class="section-label">{decision.id}</span>
                  <h3 id={`${decision.id}-heading`}>{decision.title}</h3>
                  <>
                    <p class="origin-agent">{decision.context}</p>
                    <p class="origin-supply">{supplyContexts[decision.id]}</p>
                  </>
                </>
              </div>
            </>
          </div>
          <fieldset class="incident-comparison">
            <legend>Compare the scope of the response</legend>
            <div class="incident-controls">
              <label>
                <input
                  type="radio"
                  name={`response-${decision.id}`}
                  value="both"
                  checked
                />{" "}
                Both approaches
              </label>
              {decision.options.map((option, i) => (
                <label>
                  <input
                    type="radio"
                    name={`response-${decision.id}`}
                    value={String(i)}
                  />{" "}
                  {option.name}
                </label>
              ))}
            </div>
            <div class="incident-options">
              {decision.options.map((option, i) => (
                <section
                  class="incident-option"
                  data-option={String(i)}
                  aria-label={option.name}
                >
                  <h4>{option.name}</h4>
                  <dl>
                    <dt>The strongest case</dt>
                    <dd>{option.benefit}</dd>
                    <dt>What it asks us to accept</dt>
                    <dd>{option.cost}</dd>
                    <dt>Who feels the difference</dt>
                    <dd>{option.people}</dd>
                    <dt>Evidence to ask for</dt>
                    <dd class="origin-agent">{option.evidence}</dd>
                    <dd class="origin-supply">
                      {supplyEvidence[decision.id][i]}
                    </dd>
                  </dl>
                </section>
              ))}
            </div>
          </fieldset>
          <div class="incident-obligations">
            <span>Trace this decision to</span>
            {decision.obligations.map((n) => (
              <a href={`#obligation-${n}`}>
                Obligation {String(n).padStart(2, "0")} ↗
              </a>
            ))}
          </div>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </div>
  <div class="chapter-takeaway">
    <span>THE CHECK THAT MATTERS</span><p>
      A repair is a claim.<br /><em>Resumption needs evidence.</em>
    </p><a href="#obligation-2">Obligation 02 / Match power with evidence ↗</a>
  </div>
  <p class="incident-bridge">
    Now put that decision under competitive pressure. If one lab pauses to
    investigate while another keeps moving, what makes a careful response
    sustainable? <a href="#race">The next decision is collective ↗</a>
  </p>
</section>
